{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Read down the accuracy column, then across.} The top four span 0.008 in accuracy, {\b ~130\u215 ? in predict time} (0.34 s to 89 s) and 2.4\u215 ? in memory. scANVI sits within 0.002 of second place and {\b 134\u215 ? slower than actinn-jax} at tied accuracy. The linear pipeline is both the most accurate and the fastest to fit, and pays for it in memory \u8212- 4294 vs 2399 MB \u8212- because ANOVA/PCA densify a cells \u215 ? genes matrix while actinn-jax stays sparse; that ~1.8\u215 ? ratio widens to ~2\u215 ? at atlas scale (6.1 vs 13.2 GB at 49k cells, \u167 ?3.3). The two profiles suit different jobs: one-shot labelling, versus a reference {\b trained once and reused}, where a cached model pays fit once while the linear pipeline refits scaler/PCA/classifier for every query. ProtoCloud\u8217's 146 s CPU fit buys nothing at this reference size \u8212- and buys the top accuracy at atlas scale (\u167 ?3.3).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Pretrained annotators, scored ontology-only.} The two methods with fixed label vocabularies (\u167 ?2.2, \u167 ?2.4) cannot appear in the table above, since they do not predict the dataset\u8217's label strings. They are scored by ontology concordance on the three datasets where reference and query both carry CL ids, with actinn-jax on the same splits for reference:\par}
