{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 actinn-jax {\b ties its {\f1 mlp} sibling on accuracy at ~2\u215 ? the speed} (165 vs 327 s) and {\b beats xgboost\u8217's accuracy at ~5.5\u215 ? less runtime and ~4\u215 ? less memory} (165 s / 21 GB vs 905 s / 81 GB). The faster methods (knn, logistic) are less accurate; the more accurate heavyweight (xgboost) is far slower and heavier \u8212- Pareto-non-domination {\b within Open Problems\u8217' method set}, on controlled hardware. That set contains no carefully-tuned linear pipeline, and where we add one ourselves it leads on accuracy and fit time (\u167 ?3.1), so this is a statement about the OP panel rather than a general one.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Extension: our four added methods on the same harness.} We later ported the tuned linear pipeline, scTOP, the SGD-SVM and CellTypist into Open Problems components and ran them through the same pipeline on a fresh {\f1 r7i.8xlarge} ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "results_openproblems_samehw_v2.csv"}}{\fldrslt{\ul
{\f1 results_openproblems_samehw_v2.csv}
}}}
, raw trace archived alongside). All four run correctly on a harness we did not write, which is the main thing this establishes; peak memory sits in the same 13\u8211-20 GB band as the incumbent methods (xgboost is the outlier at 55 GB). {\b We do not report cross-method runtimes from this run, and the reader should not infer them from the CSV.} Two confounds make the wall-clock numbers non-comparable: the run used a higher task concurrency (6 vs 2), and the methods differ enormously in how many cores they take \u8212- measured {\f1 %cpu} ranges from {\b 49% for CellTypist (half a core) and 117% for {\f1 mlp} (effectively single-threaded) to 2338% for the linear pipeline (23 cores)}. Wall-clock under those conditions measures threading and contention as much as algorithmic cost. It also disagrees with the controlled run above for two methods ({\f1 mlp} 327 s \u8594 ? 769 s, {\f1 cellmapper_linear} 58 s \u8594 ? 717 s), which we flag rather than reconcile: the table above, run at low concurrency, remains the cost comparison we stand behind. Coverage was partial when a wall-clock budget stopped the run \u8212- six datasets for the SVM and CellTypist, four for scTOP, three for the linear pipeline \u8212- so per-method means over different dataset subsets would not be comparable either.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Three cheap ablations, and their limits.} (i) {\i Input standardization} \u8212- z-scoring genes to the reference\u8217's frozen mean/std, a CPU-only domain-alignment inspired by scArches reference surgery \u8212- lifts mean accuracy +0.2 and macro-F1 +1.2 pt (largest on batch-shifted datasets: gtex macro-F1 +7.9). It is shipped {\b opt-in} ({\f1 standardize=True}) because it shifts the softmax calibration that the two-stage abstain thresholds are tuned against. (ii) {\i Gene budget} \u8212- OP feeds every method 1000 HVGs, which most starves a gene-space MLP. Widening to ~5000 HVGs lifts accuracy on 4 of 6 datasets (immune/gtex +3 pt) and there {\b matches scANVI+scArches\u8217's full-config leaderboard accuracy} (immune 0.891 \u8776 ? 0.892) on CPU in minutes \u8212- the gap to the top method was largely a gene-budget artifact, not the VAE. But more genes is {\b not} a universal win: it {\i regresses} tabula_sapiens by ~10 pt (its 284-cell test batch across 160 fine types overfits reference-specific genes) and saturates hypomap. Crucially, this is {\b selectable without test labels}: held-out {\i reference} cross-validation rises for the datasets that benefit and is the one signal that {\i drops} for tabula_sapiens, and a trivial query-cells-per-class check independently flags it \u8212- so the budget can be set deterministically per dataset. (iii) {\i Negative control} \u8212- a CPU-only, UCE-style protein-embedding featurization (expression-weighted mean of ESM2 gene embeddings) does {\b not} help and hurts the hardest case: the pooling discards the per-gene detail that separates fine types, so the value of a foundation model like UCE stays locked in its GPU transformer, not a portable averaging trick.\par}
